#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6ecc42-d84a-584f-9ccc-d6a987c89394" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "0b823033-1f76-5656-a284-b94f33f3e359" :question/math-academy-id "e-12828" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2},\\qquad \\sqrt{8},\\qquad \\sqrt{16}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{2}=1.414\\,213\\,562\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{8}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{8}=2.828\\,427\\,124\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{16}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $16$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16}=\\sqrt{4^2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{8}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c606764-287a-500f-aa91-e6a68bec8860" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Decimal Approximation of a Square Root Surd"}] :question/id #uuid "127ac025-aa32-505a-98d8-27721a37fdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-12829" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sqrt{126}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126}=11.224\\,972\\,160\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126} \\approx 11.225\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "115ee606-48f2-5a63-a5c6-fd8e3e1dbec0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cube Root Surds"}] :question/id #uuid "9e145904-a9bf-59fc-9070-339b0e133de9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12830" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{11}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$, cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{\\phantom{0}}, \\sqrt[5]{\\phantom{0}},\\ldots,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1.259\\,921\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $27$ are perfect cubes, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.223\\,980\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1e6bea-5399-5dba-b2b1-f59441915324" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "12b5f86f-9e86-5c1b-8773-38e2b69a6417" :question/math-academy-id "e-13357" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}} = 0.645\\,497\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\cdot 8}{9\\cdot 9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{8}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}} = 1.195\\,228\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c606764-287a-500f-aa91-e6a68bec8860" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Decimal Approximation of a Square Root Surd"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e51721d-28a8-5621-adf0-f7fdfc0ed31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e51721d-28a8-5621-adf0-f7fdfc0ed31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476269} :answer-field/id #uuid "67eb52de-60f6-5fe4-9838-37cb5e98e935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c21e2314-9d15-5091-bdd2-228be712839c" :question/math-academy-id "q-175535" :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{21}$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?\n\n$\\sqrt{21}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21}=4.582\\,575\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round the value to $1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21}≈4.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1e6bea-5399-5dba-b2b1-f59441915324" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ee73ea2-fcf5-5ba2-adce-e97835d53a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476307} {:answer/id #uuid "5492c3ef-d4fd-540f-a1b2-71d537e30546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476382} {:answer/id #uuid "5994c656-0d9e-549a-87be-5337acd2a039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476398} {:answer/id #uuid "5be02b08-58e1-59e1-a7a8-03f5f47b147f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476406} {:answer/id #uuid "be98014a-eb32-58bf-b690-bb748d046b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be02b08-58e1-59e1-a7a8-03f5f47b147f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476406} :answer-field/id #uuid "14a9c18a-d25b-5c63-8e26-e67c5d82248a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07640a37-cc54-5ef1-91c5-4812836d51a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-185159" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}$ is a surd because $\\frac{1}{6}$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}=0.408\\,248\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{9\\cdot 9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $100$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{81}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{100}}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1e6bea-5399-5dba-b2b1-f59441915324" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61f9fb53-6162-58de-8f4b-0b9718c6e4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476425} {:answer/id #uuid "726cd19d-936b-57f4-8516-1a8b1d84c092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476462} {:answer/id #uuid "93abd272-3a7e-52a3-a1f6-d21de4a731a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476520} {:answer/id #uuid "aac63a8e-dae4-5881-928c-6e018e74a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476581} {:answer/id #uuid "b64702f9-e78c-544c-8ffe-6f7dac2e1b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64702f9-e78c-544c-8ffe-6f7dac2e1b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476617} :answer-field/id #uuid "646ff995-7100-5862-a820-4803a6a1b3a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a01f34bc-501b-51fb-84a4-4db1dd9276f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-185183" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $36$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot 6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $9$ and $100$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{10}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{10\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}$ is a surd because $\\frac{11}{16}$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}}=0.829\\,156\\,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{100}}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6ecc42-d84a-584f-9ccc-d6a987c89394" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f69457-ca71-5ee7-8ad1-62c7d2a5a301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{64}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476283} {:answer/id #uuid "2340ddaa-e741-5b5e-8571-8951ce7ced21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{49}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476284} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d4fe12-253e-50e5-82b9-0aaba5ae3f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{28}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476643} {:answer/id #uuid "c66540f4-3820-5527-8a0a-ac810f8bc513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{28}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476650} {:answer/id #uuid "f12d83e7-e6a1-592d-a839-783faaaf7cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f12d83e7-e6a1-592d-a839-783faaaf7cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476738} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6eb96f-7bc1-580e-b3e0-38494dc9daf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6be9ff93-7c80-5a18-be7e-bd70edb8b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43325" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are **NOT** surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49},\\,\\sqrt{28},\\,\\sqrt{64}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{49}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $49$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{}$) and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49}=\\sqrt{{7}^{2}}=7\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{28}$ is a surd because $28$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{28}=5.291\\,502\\,622\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{64}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{64}=\\sqrt{{8}^{2}}=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{49}$ and $\\sqrt{64}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6ecc42-d84a-584f-9ccc-d6a987c89394" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7cf0f8-2065-5fa3-aaca-8888cf2f2375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{144}$ and $\\sqrt{25}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476228} {:answer/id #uuid "4f90389a-c43c-535a-8b7c-b92d22402df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{25}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476366} {:answer/id #uuid "9efadb26-e3a1-5431-b78a-da0f0df152bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{74}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476545} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9c95ea-29bf-52e7-926b-c1939f41934c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{144}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476659} {:answer/id #uuid "cadda449-489a-591b-808e-2668fec8b13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{74}$ and $\\sqrt{144}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9efadb26-e3a1-5431-b78a-da0f0df152bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{74}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476545} :answer-field/id #uuid "698f5835-2eab-512c-8baa-0ec2ce770ede" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aba568b7-e4b5-5a07-85ab-7669ffe98d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-43540" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{144},\\,\\sqrt{25},\\,\\sqrt{74}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{144}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since  $144$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{144}=\\sqrt{{12}^{2}}=12\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{25}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $25$  is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{})$ and obtain a whole number: \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{25}=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}}=5\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{74}$ is a surd because $74$ is not a perfect square. In fact $\\sqrt{74}=8.602\\text{ }325\\,...$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{74}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c606764-287a-500f-aa91-e6a68bec8860" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Decimal Approximation of a Square Root Surd"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aa40417-a2cd-5cf5-9fbe-75007fdeeb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.338" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476403} {:answer/id #uuid "a91e6b96-38c6-5f86-b7fa-5d449fd615e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476574} {:answer/id #uuid "cf636510-9442-555d-95aa-c7902cc47efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476666} {:answer/id #uuid "d607aeb9-2810-5b55-bbfb-0bdf44ed37e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476685} {:answer/id #uuid "dacae3d7-59dd-5058-925e-c95633811db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91e6b96-38c6-5f86-b7fa-5d449fd615e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476574} :answer-field/id #uuid "99421956-99fc-5fe4-be06-4827cb477760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93bd4c15-92a4-568f-aaec-3b88b27585e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71355" :question/problem "What is $\\sqrt{42}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{42}=6.480\\,740\\,698\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round the value to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{42}≈6.481.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "115ee606-48f2-5a63-a5c6-fd8e3e1dbec0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cube Root Surds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14971dd6-fe9f-51a5-8502-501597d33911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{10}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476236} {:answer/id #uuid "50016bcc-3df6-5f94-9682-fbb4a337e3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476367} {:answer/id #uuid "638a7df1-c304-5b92-ba85-ed2e8e9763a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{100}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476428} {:answer/id #uuid "87f28ce3-7b3a-5e93-988c-bfdc464705d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{10}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476501} {:answer/id #uuid "a0be7714-7090-58d1-8ebb-4cc553fd3db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{100}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14971dd6-fe9f-51a5-8502-501597d33911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{10}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476236} :answer-field/id #uuid "784ea43f-add1-5fb2-ab43-e164314a73c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7293d832-30bd-529a-a59d-7e39970dea7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71358" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10},\\,\\sqrt[3]{100},\\,\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{},$ cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{},$ $\\sqrt[5]{},…,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ is a surd because $10$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{10}=2.154\\,434\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ is a surd because $100$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{100}=4.641\\,588\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1\\,000$ is a perfect cube, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{})$ and obtain a whole number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} & =\\sqrt[3]{{10}^{3}}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{10}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{100}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "115ee606-48f2-5a63-a5c6-fd8e3e1dbec0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cube Root Surds"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d7ea31-a2a4-5aba-9002-c6de8ea5a613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{125}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476207} {:answer/id #uuid "23ed40eb-b855-55a1-9064-5790cd0a71cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{125}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476286} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4e6ecd-3292-53b5-b320-3dfc90d23277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{50}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{125}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476449} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea19d4-7cef-56d5-a874-3fc7fc4ff331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476577} {:answer/id #uuid "d84ac485-d200-598d-a618-cf0b9185b86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ea19d4-7cef-56d5-a874-3fc7fc4ff331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476577} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da85938-8c8f-5559-a0d5-b70ec08cc095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e15be35a-fdf2-54f2-a053-e59892bfa1cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71361" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{5},\\,\\sqrt[3]{50},\\,\\sqrt[3]{125}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{},$ cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{},$ $\\sqrt[5]{},…,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is a surd because $5$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{5}=1.709\\,975\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ is a surd because $50$ is not a perfect cube. In fact $\\sqrt[3]{50}=3.684\\,031\\,…$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{125}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $125$ is a perfect cube, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{})$ and obtain a whole number.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{125} & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{50}$ only."}]}
